{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"tangible user interface tangible user interface","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md","fileName":"Tangible User Interface.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Tangible User Interface","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 13:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Human-Computer Interaction Foundations]] [[Tangible and Multimodal Interaction]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Tangible and Multimodal Interaction","Embodied Interaction","Augmented Reality","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"tangible user interface tangible user interface","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md","fileName":"Tangible User Interface.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Tangible User Interface","heading":"Tangible User Interface","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A tangible user interface uses physical objects to represent digital information and to provide mechanisms for interacting with it. Computation can be distributed among objects that are aware of their location or proximity to other objects.\n\nBecause the physical representation and the control are integrated, users can apply existing skills for handling real-world objects. Multiple spatially aware objects can also support concurrent access and manipulation.\n\nTangible interfaces are a form of [[Embodied Interaction]] and can overlap with [[Augmented Reality]] when physical objects are enhanced by digital information.\n\nIn AR, tangibles may have generic shapes whose tracked pose controls virtual content or distinctive shapes whose physical form identifies their role. The object supplies grasping, contact, and shared-space cues, while augmentation adds changeable appearance and behavior that the physical artifact cannot provide alone.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Tangible and Multimodal Interaction","Embodied Interaction","Augmented Reality","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"tangible user interface tangible user interface","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Tangible User Interface.md","fileName":"Tangible User Interface.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Tangible User Interface","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf]]\n[[augmentedreality_pearson.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Human-Computer Interaction Foundations","Tangible and Multimodal Interaction","Embodied Interaction","Augmented Reality","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}